cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Double Torus — A Deterministic Quantum Simulation Kernel with Content-Addressed Verification"
abstract: >-
  A dependency-free, zero-egress computational kernel for deterministic quantum
  circuit simulation and content-addressed verification.

  Measured results, reproduced by execution on 2026-08-20: Grover's algorithm
  passes 4/4 tests with 94.5–99.9% success probability on databases of size
  8, 16 and 32, at a measured 4.00×–8.00× speedup over classical linear search;
  Shor's algorithm returns 4/4 correct factorizations (15, 21, 33, 77) via
  genuine order-finding, though it runs SLOWER than classical trial division
  (0.15×–0.34×) and is offered as a correctness result only. The kernel is
  2,131 lines with zero imports and zero network calls. The geometry is a
  genus-2 double torus, Euler characteristic χ = −2. A sealed ledger of 1,329
  entries includes 36 machine-checked involution theorems, against which claims
  are adjudicated by citation.

  This work does NOT prove, solve, or resolve any Clay Millennium Prize Problem.
  Earlier drafts asserted such proofs; they were withdrawn after audit and the
  reasoning is recorded in HONESTY.md. The Lean sources do not currently compile
  against Mathlib and are not offered as formal verification. No production
  readiness score, certification grade, or coverage percentage is claimed.

  Full audit with commands, outputs and receipts: RELEASE-AUDIT-uuidna.md

